        "cards": [
         {
          "title": "What it is for",
          "body_md": "Whenever we move about — walking, coming and going — we might hurt tiny living beings without noticing. **Iriyavahiyam** (ઇરિયાવહિયં) is how we say sorry for that before we begin Samayik.",
          "emoji": "🐜"
         },
         {
          "title": "Little lives on the path",
          "body_md": "It names them: seeds, green sprouts, dew drops, ant hills, moss, water, damp earth and spider webs. Every one of them is a living being.",
          "emoji": "🌿"
         },
         {
          "title": "Counting by senses",
          "body_md": "One sense (*ekendriya*): earth, water, fire, air and plants. Two: worms and shell creatures. Three: ants. Four: bees and flies. Five: fish, birds, animals and people.",
          "emoji": "🔢"
         },
         {
          "title": "First line and ending",
          "body_md": "It starts by asking permission: *“Ichchhakarena sandisaha bhagavan! Iriyavahiyam padikkamami? Ichchham.”* It ends with *“tassa michchhami dukkadam”* — may that wrong be undone.",
          "emoji": "🕊️"
         }
        ],
        "fun_fact": "We do Iriyavahiyam before Samayik, Pratikraman and Chaityavandan — like wiping the slate clean before writing.",
